 ## Overview
-The Symmetric Matrix Data Structure is a compact memory layout for representing $3 \times 3$ symmetric matrices by storing only the six unique elements. It solves the problem of redundant storage in symmetric matrix operations, reducing memory footprint by 50% compared to a full $3 \times 3$ matrix. It sets itself apart from general matrix structures by enforcing symmetry constraints implicitly through its field layout, ensuring $m_{01}=m_{10}$, $m_{02}=m_{20}$, and $m_{12}=m_{21}$ without requiring runtime checks or extra fields.
+A compact storage format for 3x3 symmetric matrices, reducing the required memory from nine values to six by exploiting the property $M_{ij} = M_{ji}$. It serves as the foundational data structure for representing covariance matrices in Gaussian Splatting variants and stress tensors in scientific visualization, eliminating redundant storage and ensuring symmetry constraints are maintained by construction.
 
